(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatology II Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which feature separates papulopustular rosacea from acne vulgaris?","choices":["Rosacea has no comedones","Rosacea produces pustules and acne vulgaris does not","Rosacea occurs only in patients over the age of sixty","Rosacea spares the central face entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — open or closed comedones point to acne vulgaris instead. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is used for suppressive therapy in recurrent herpes-associated erythema multiforme?","choices":["Dapsone 25 to 200 mg daily with regular monitoring of the blood count","Hydroxychloroquine 200 mg twice daily taken indefinitely","Prednisone 40 to 60 mg daily for five days at each recurrence","Acyclovir 400 mg twice daily or valacyclovir 500 mg daily"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dapsone or hydroxychloroquine are used for refractory non-herpes recurrent disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which systemic therapy has the strongest current evidence in toxic epidermal necrolysis?","choices":["Prophylactic antibiotics covering skin and gut flora","Cyclosporine at 3 to 5 mg/kg daily, started early","Intravenous immunoglobulin at 1 g/kg daily for three days","High-dose systemic corticosteroids given for the first week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with intravenous immunoglobulin and etanercept as adjunct options. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which investigation excludes phaeochromocytoma as a cause of secondary hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Fasting glucose with haemoglobin A1c","Twenty-four hour urine metanephrines and catecholamines","Thyroid-stimulating hormone with anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — episodic sweating with headache, palpitations and hypertension is the clue. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which single feature most usefully distinguishes erythema nodosum from nodular vasculitis?","choices":["Erythema nodosum nodules occur exclusively in men","Erythema nodosum nodules do not ulcerate","Erythema nodosum nodules appear only on the posterior legs","Erythema nodosum nodules are painless on palpation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they sit on the anterior shins whereas nodular vasculitis favours the posterior legs and ulcerates. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which topical agent is the only one approved for photoaging, and how does it work?","choices":["Niacinamide, which reduces inflammation and improves skin sallowness","Tretinoin, which stimulates collagen synthesis and inhibits matrix metalloproteinase activity","Hydroquinone, which inhibits melanin production within the epidermis","Vitamin C, which neutralises reactive oxygen species and stimulates new collagen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, requiring a minimum of six to twelve months for visible results. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is the gold standard test for determining the cleavage plane in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Serum enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay for structural protein antibodies","Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin G","Genetic testing of the known structural protein genes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with immunofluorescence antigen mapping used to localise missing or reduced proteins. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which type of epidermolysis bullosa is most common, and what is its cleavage level?","choices":["Junctional epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with cleavage in the lamina lucida","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with sub-lamina densa cleavage","Kindler epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with mixed cleavage planes","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, at about 70% of cases, with intraepidermal cleavage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it results from keratin 5 and 14 mutations and is autosomal dominant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What distinguishes erythema multiforme minor from erythema multiforme major?","choices":["Minor is drug-triggered; major is triggered by herpes simplex virus","Minor occurs in children; major occurs only in adults over forty","Minor involves skin only; major involves two or more mucosal surfaces","Minor lasts under one week; major lasts more than one month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erythema multiforme minor constitutes the majority of cases. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which epidermolysis bullosa subtype carries the highest mortality?","choices":["Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, especially the localised palmoplantar form","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, especially the dominant form","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita, the autoimmune acquired form","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa, especially the Herlitz subtype"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why early palliative care integration is appropriate in severe Herlitz disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which test is mandatory when investigating polymorphous light eruption?","choices":["A porphyrin screen of urine, stool and red cells in every patient","Photopatch testing with the standard photoallergen series","Skin biopsy, which shows a pathognomonic pattern","An antinuclear antibody panel, to exclude lupus erythematosus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, especially anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which rosacea subtype is most common, and what defines it?","choices":["Erythematotelangiectatic, with persistent central facial erythema, flushing and telangiectasias but no papules or pustules","Papulopustular, with transient central facial papules and pustules on a background of erythema and no comedones","Phymatous, with skin thickening and nodular enlargement of the nose and chin","Ocular, with blepharitis, conjunctivitis and meibomian gland dysfunction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the skin is often described as sensitive and stinging. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which biopsy finding is described as pathognomonic across the Stevens-Johnson and toxic epidermal necrolysis spectrum?","choices":["Granular immunoglobulin A deposited within the dermal papillae of the skin","Palisading granulomas surrounding an area of necrobiosis","Septal panniculitis without any accompanying vasculitis","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with dermal-epidermal junction separation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, on punch biopsy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which pharmacogenomic association carries direct pre-prescription implications?","choices":["HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8, for sulfonamide antibiotics","HLA-Cw6 in European populations, for aromatic anticonvulsants","HLA-A31 and HLA-BW35, for nevirapine","HLA-B*15:02 in Asian populations, for carbamazepine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with HLA-B*58:01 associated with allopurinol. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which wavelength band is primarily responsible for sunburn, and by what mechanism?","choices":["Ultraviolet A at 320 to 400 nm, causing direct DNA damage through pyrimidine dimers","Visible light at 400 to 700 nm, causing thermal injury to the epidermis","Infrared radiation, causing collagen denaturation within the dermis","Ultraviolet B at 290 to 320 nm, causing direct DNA damage through cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with ultraviolet A contributing through oxidative stress and indirect DNA damage. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which haplotypes are strongly associated with dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["HLA-Cw6 and HLA-B27","HLA-A31 and HLA-BW35","HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8","HLA-B*15:02 and HLA-B*58:01"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and nearly all patients have underlying gluten-sensitive enteropathy even when gastrointestinally asymptomatic. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which drug classes are classic causes of phototoxicity?","choices":["Aromatic anticonvulsants, allopurinol and nevirapine","Oral contraceptives, sulfonamides and bromides","Tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, amiodarone and thiazides","Sunscreen chemicals, sulfonamides and phenothiazines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside furosemide, voriconazole, some nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and psoralens. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which finding should prompt a search for hereditary angio-oedema?","choices":["Recurrent angio-oedema occurring without any wheals","Wheals that persist beyond twenty-four hours and leave bruising","Wheals accompanied by Darier's sign and an elevated tryptase","Wheals appearing only after exposure to cold or vibration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — check complement C4 and C1-esterase inhibitor level and function. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which biologic is approved for refractory chronic urticaria?","choices":["Dupilumab, an interleukin-4 receptor antagonist given subcutaneously","Etanercept, a tumour necrosis factor inhibitor given subcutaneously","Omalizumab, an anti-immunoglobulin E antibody given every four weeks","Infliximab, a tumour necrosis factor alpha inhibitor given intravenously"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, at 300 mg subcutaneously. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the hallmark histopathologic finding of dermatoheliosis?","choices":["Solar elastosis, the replacement of normal collagen with thickened tangled elastin","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with dermal-epidermal junction separation","Palisading granulomas with central mucin deposition","Interface dermatitis with vacuolar basal layer degeneration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside epidermal atrophy with loss of rete ridges and vascular ectasia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about patients with Fitzpatrick types IV to VI?","choices":["They should use SPF 15 rather than SPF 30, on the basis that higher factors confer no additional benefit","They are not immune to ultraviolet damage or skin cancer, and melanoma is frequently diagnosed at advanced stages","They carry no meaningful skin cancer risk and need no routine photoprotection","They burn more readily than types I to III but tan more deeply afterwards"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — delayed diagnosis is common because of a lower index of suspicion. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"How is an isolated actinic keratosis usually treated?","choices":["Cryotherapy with two to three freeze-thaw cycles","5-fluorouracil cream applied for two to four weeks","Imiquimod applied twice weekly for sixteen weeks","Photodynamic therapy using aminolevulinic acid and light"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the standard of care for isolated lesions, with shave excision or curettage as alternatives. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"What does biopsy show in granuloma annulare?","choices":["A dense neutrophilic infiltrate at the ulcer edge","Interface dermatitis with scattered necrotic keratinocytes","Palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis and mucin deposition","Septal panniculitis without vasculitis and Miescher's granulomas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — thought to involve a type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction around degenerating collagen. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which systemic association is present in a quarter to a half of pyoderma gangrenosum cases?","choices":["Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance","Inflammatory bowel disease","Rheumatoid arthritis and seronegative arthritis","Haematologic malignancy such as acute myeloid leukaemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and peristomal disease occurs in up to 15% of patients with intestinal ostomies and bowel disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How are acute and chronic urticaria distinguished, and how common is chronic disease?","choices":["Acute lasts under six weeks; chronic exceeds six weeks and affects 0.5 to 1% of the population","Acute lasts under twenty-four hours; chronic exceeds twenty-four hours in any single lesion","Acute lasts under two weeks; chronic exceeds two weeks and affects 15 to 25% of people","Acute is immunoglobulin E mediated; chronic is always autoimmune in origin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lifetime prevalence of urticaria overall is 15 to 25%. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which organism contributes to papulopustular rosacea?","choices":["Demodex folliculorum, whose overgrowth drives inflammation","Malassezia, whose overgrowth drives inflammation in sebum-rich areas","Staphylococcus aureus, colonising the follicles","Cutibacterium acnes, proliferating within blocked follicles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why ivermectin 1% cream outperforms metronidazole in Demodex-associated disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which test is the gold standard for diagnosing dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Small bowel biopsy demonstrating villous atrophy consistent with coeliac disease","Perilesional skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence showing granular immunoglobulin A in the dermal papillae","Lesional skin biopsy with haematoxylin and eosin showing subepidermal blistering with neutrophils","Serum immunoglobulin A anti-tissue transglutaminase with anti-endomysial antibodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immunoglobulin A anti-epidermal transglutaminase is the most specific serological test. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which single intervention most improves survival in Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","choices":["Prophylactic broad-spectrum antibiotics started on admission","High-dose systemic corticosteroids given throughout the admission","Immediate withdrawal of the causative drug, since each day of delay worsens prognosis","Early administration of intravenous immunoglobulin at 1 g/kg daily for three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which counselling point is emphasised for acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["It is a metabolic warning sign rather than a hygiene problem, and 5 to 10% weight loss improves the findings","It is a benign cosmetic change that carries no systemic implication and requires no further investigation","It responds fully to topical antifungal therapy within several weeks","It resolves spontaneously in most patients without any intervention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the hygiene misconception is specifically addressed. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which group carries markedly increased actinic keratosis risk?","choices":["Patients with inflammatory bowel disease, at roughly 10 times the risk","Patients with coeliac disease, at roughly 30 times the risk","Organ transplant recipients, at roughly 65 times the risk","Patients with atopic dermatitis, at roughly 20 times the risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside Fitzpatrick types I and II, male sex, age over fifty and outdoor occupation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"}]};
